    ('02d-decisions-delivery.md',
     '**Choice**: Metered spend on everything beyond the fixed B1 plan is capped at $10/month by governance: exceeding it requires a constitution amendment. An Azure budget on the subscription notifies the owner at $7/month and again at $10/month; an Azure budget sends alerts and does not stop resources. The Application Insights daily ingestion cap of 150 MB is the one configured hard limit, because it is the only metered service in this design that offers one (Constitution VIII.4). The B1 plan is the fixed cost.\n**Rationale**: Constitution VIII.1 requires that metered spend not exceed $10/month and that a budget alert fire at $7/month. Constitution VIII.4 requires that every metered service offering a hard spending limit have one configured inside the VIII.1 cap. Application Insights offers a daily ingestion cap; 150 MB/day keeps monthly ingestion well inside the 5 GB/month free allowance at about 4.5 GB/month worst case (research R-09; decisions #6).\n**Alternatives**: The owner was offered caps of $2 (alert $1.50), $5 (alert $3), $10 (alert $7), or no cap, and chose $10 with a $7 alert in the hosting interview (decisions #6). A higher daily Application Insights cap was rejected because 150 MB/day already keeps monthly ingestion inside the 5 GB free allowance (research R-09).\n**Pivot cost**: low: changing the cap requires an amendment to Constitution VIII.1 and an update to the Azure budget rule; neither touches application code (decisions #6).',
     '**Choice**: Metered spend on everything beyond the fixed B2 plan is capped at $10/month by governance: exceeding it requires a constitution amendment. No budget alert is configured: the owner withdrew it ("No alert needed"; constitution 6.0.0; 012 decisions #21). The Application Insights daily ingestion cap of 0.15 GB (150 MB) on `portfolio-appinsights` is the only configured hard limit, because it is the only metered service in this design that offers one (Constitution VIII.4). The B2 plan, US$26.28/month at list price, is the fixed cost and has no metered component (012 research R-12).\n**Rationale**: Constitution VIII.1 (6.0.0) caps metered spend at $10/month and no longer requires a spending alert; Constitution VIII.3 keeps the projected metered total under $7/month; Constitution VIII.4 requires that every metered service offering a hard spending limit have one configured inside the VIII.1 cap. Application Insights offers a daily ingestion cap; 150 MB/day keeps monthly ingestion well inside the 5 GB/month free allowance at about 4.5 GB/month worst case (research R-09; decisions #6). Nothing notifies the owner if Azure spend rises; this is bounded in practice because the plan is fixed-price and the ingestion cap limits the only metered Azure line (012 decisions #21).\n**Alternatives**: The owner was offered caps of $2 (alert $1.50), $5 (alert $3), $10 (alert $7), or no cap, and chose $10 with a $7 alert in the hosting interview (decisions #6). That Azure budget alert at $7 and $10 was withdrawn by the owner in feature 012 (constitution 6.0.0); one budget on the resource group restores it with no code change. A higher daily Application Insights cap was rejected because 150 MB/day already keeps monthly ingestion inside the 5 GB free allowance (research R-09).\n**Pivot cost**: low: changing the cap requires an amendment to Constitution VIII.1, and restoring an alert is one Azure budget in the portal; neither touches application code (012 decisions #21).',
     1),
    ('02d-decisions-delivery.md',
     "### D-24: Infrastructure as Code\n**Area**: infrastructure-as-code\n**Status**: chosen\n**Choice**: OpenTofu (the open-source Terraform fork, installed with `brew install opentofu`, run as `tofu`) manages every Azure, Cloudflare (including R2), and MongoDB Atlas resource from one root module in `infra/`. Since constitution 4.0.0 this decision is on hold: hosting is under review and the site is built locally (docs/roadmap.md); the R2 buckets are created by hand until then. The module has no sub-modules and uses four providers: `azurerm`, `azuread` (the app registration and GitHub federated credential for D-25), `cloudflare`, and `mongodbatlas`. It has one environment, production. Files: `main.tf` (providers and backend), `azure.tf` (B1 plan and Web App with Always On, system managed identity, app settings, and custom domain; Origin CA certificate binding; access restrictions built from the `cloudflare_ip_ranges` data source; Log Analytics and Application Insights with a 150 MB daily cap; a subscription budget alerting at $7 and $10), `cloudflare.tf` (site and `img.<domain>` DNS, the `asuid` TXT record, Full (strict) TLS, Always Use HTTPS, Authenticated Origin Pulls, the Origin CA certificate, cache rules, Tiered Cache, Early Hints, the two R2 buckets with their custom domain, CORS, and lifecycle rules, a bucket-scoped R2 token, and a purge-only API token), `atlas.tf` (M0 cluster, database user, and an access list of the Web App's possible outbound IPs), `deploy.tf` (the D-25 app registration, federated credential, and role assignment), plus `variables.tf` and `outputs.tf`. Cross-provider values (hostnames, verification ids, IP ranges, the connection string, and the purge token) flow between resources inside one plan. State lives in a dedicated small storage account and `tfstate` container, created once with `az` before the first `tofu init` and locked by blob lease. Secrets are App Service app settings written by OpenTofu; there is no Key Vault. Steps with no usable API stay in a runbook (`infra/README.md`): adding the domain to Cloudflare, creating the Google OAuth client, and creating the provisioning credentials (Azure CLI login, a Cloudflare provisioning token, and Atlas API keys), which stay on the owner's machine and never reach the app. OpenTofu runs only from the owner's machine (`tofu plan`, then `tofu apply`); the D-25 pipeline never runs it.\n**Rationale**: Accepted from proposal P-02 option A by the owner. The deciding factor is the cross-provider wiring: the Web App hostname and verification id feed Cloudflare DNS, Cloudflare's IP ranges feed Azure access restrictions (Constitution, Platform Constraints: origin accepts only Cloudflare), outbound IPs feed the Atlas access list, and three secrets feed app settings. One plan wires and previews all of it and detects drift when a dashboard setting is edited. OpenTofu was chosen over HashiCorp Terraform because Homebrew core no longer ships Terraform after its licence change, while OpenTofu installs with one official command and uses the same providers and language (Constitution VI). The state account is separate from the app's storage account because the app account is itself created by the plan, so it cannot hold the state for its own first apply. The budget and the ingestion cap are provisioned with the first resources because metered spend starts then (Constitution VIII.1, VIII.4).\n**Alternatives**: Bicep through `az` for Azure, with a curl script for Cloudflare and the `atlas` CLI, was not chosen: native and stateless, but the cross-provider wiring becomes hand glue between three imperative scripts, Cloudflare changes cannot be previewed, and nothing re-syncs the IP ranges. Dashboards plus a checklist were not chosen: there is nothing to rebuild from or diff against. HashiCorp Terraform was not chosen only because of installation; the files are compatible. Storing state in the app's own storage account (as P-02 first proposed) was rejected because of the bootstrap order. Key Vault was rejected: metered per operation, and one more component for three secrets. Running OpenTofu in GitHub Actions (plan-only or plan-and-apply) was rejected by the owner: Cloudflare and Atlas tokens would have to be stored as GitHub secrets, and the pipeline identity would need subscription-wide rights to create role assignments. Infrastructure changes are rare enough that a local run costs little.\n**Pivot cost**: low before provisioning; after, moving to another tool is a re-import of about 40 resources (about an hour), and moving to dashboards is simply deleting `infra/` and the state account.",
     '### D-24: Infrastructure as Code (Withdrawn: Runbook and Settings Check)\n**Area**: infrastructure-as-code\n**Status**: chosen\n**Choice**: Withdrawn by the owner in feature 012 ("I\'m just making it by hand"; 012 decisions #4): no infrastructure-as-code tool manages production. Every resource is made by hand by the owner from the ordered runbook `infra/README.md`, in which each step names exact values and ends with a check line, except the Atlas project, cluster, user and access list, which the committed script `infra/atlas.sh` creates with the official Atlas CLI (D-05). Drift is detected by `infra/check.sh`, a read-only settings check pinned to the subscription and resource group `portfolio`: it reads with `az ... show|list`, HTTP GET, `dig` and `curl`, prints one `PASS` or `FAIL` line per setting (secrets as presence and length only), and exits non-zero on any FAIL. Cloudflare and R2 settings are checked by their observable outcome, with no Cloudflare API token (constitution 6.1.0 V.4; 012 decisions #27). The Cloudflare IP ranges are fetched live, so a range change shows up as a FAIL on the access-restriction row. Secrets are App Service app settings entered by the owner in the portal; there is no Key Vault.\n**Rationale**: The owner chose to build production by hand. A runbook plus a read-only check keeps what infrastructure-as-code was chosen for, exact repeatable values and drift detection when a dashboard setting is edited, without a state store or provisioning credentials kept anywhere (Constitution VI prefers official CLIs, used by `infra/atlas.sh`; Constitution V.4 proportional effort). Owner step O-08 proves drift detection live by turning one real setting off and seeing its row FAIL (012 research R-10).\n**Alternatives**: OpenTofu over one root module with the Azure, Cloudflare and Atlas providers and state in a dedicated storage account, accepted from proposal P-02 option A and on hold since constitution 4.0.0, was withdrawn by the owner in feature 012. Bicep through `az` with a curl script for Cloudflare was not chosen: the cross-provider wiring becomes hand glue and Cloudflare changes cannot be previewed. Dashboards with a checklist alone were rejected: there is nothing to diff against, which the read-only check now supplies. A check self-test with recorded fixtures and Cloudflare API rows behind a read-only token were dropped under Constitution V.4, because each was a second proof of something already proven (012 decisions #27). Key Vault was rejected: metered per operation, and one more component for a handful of secrets.\n**Pivot cost**: low: the check rows map one to one to resources if infrastructure-as-code returns (012 decisions #4).',
     1),
    ('02d-decisions-delivery.md',
     "### D-25: Deployment Pipeline\n**Area**: deployment-pipeline\n**Status**: chosen\n**Choice**: GitHub Actions in the `zzacal/port` repository, defined in one workflow file. Every push to any branch restores, builds, and runs both test projects. Pushes to `main` then publish with `dotnet publish` and deploy to the Web App with `azure/webapps-deploy`, after the tests pass. The workflow signs in to Azure with `azure/login` using OIDC federation: OpenTofu (D-24) creates an Entra app registration with a federated credential whose subject is the `main` branch of `zzacal/port`, and a role assignment scoped to the Web App only. The three non-secret ids the login needs (client, tenant, subscription) are stored as GitHub repository variables with `gh variable set`; no credential is stored in GitHub. There is one environment and no staging slot. Integration tests that need real Mongo and Azurite run on the hosted runner, which has Docker available, when the first feature that adds them arrives.\n**Rationale**: Accepted from proposal P-03 by the owner. Tests on every push catch a broken branch before the local merge into `main`, and deploying only from `main` matches the owner's workflow of merging locally and pushing. OIDC means no long-lived secret exists to leak or rotate. Scoping the role to the Web App limits what a compromised workflow can do. B1 has no deployment slots, and slots would need Standard S1, which exceeds Constitution VIII.1.\n**Alternatives**: Running the workflow only on `main` was not chosen because branches would go untested until merge. Deploying from the owner's machine with `az webapp deploy` was not chosen because no test gate would stand between a commit and production. It remains the manual fallback. A publish profile or client secret in GitHub secrets was rejected in favour of OIDC.\n**Pivot cost**: low: the pipeline is one workflow file plus one app registration, and switching to manual `az webapp deploy` needs no infrastructure change.",
     "### D-25: Deployment Pipeline\n**Area**: deployment-pipeline\n**Status**: chosen\n**Choice**: GitHub Actions in the `zzacal/port` repository, workflow `.github/workflows/ci.yml`. A `test` job runs the full suite on every push to any branch, on `macos-latest`, where Homebrew installs the encoders at the paths the tests expect, Google Chrome is preinstalled, and Docker runs through Colima. A `deploy` job runs only on `refs/heads/main`, `needs: test`, and deploys the artifact the test job uploads, so the deployed bits are the tested bits, with `azure/webapps-deploy` to `portfolio-web`. It signs in with `azure/login` using OIDC as the user-assigned managed identity `portfolio-deploy`, whose federated credential subject is `repo:zzacal/port:ref:refs/heads/main` and whose only role is Website Contributor on `portfolio-web`. The deploy job declares no `environment:`, because an environment changes the OIDC subject and the login would fail. The three non-secret ids (`AZURE_CLIENT_ID`, `AZURE_TENANT_ID`, `AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID`) are GitHub repository variables; no credential is stored in GitHub, and basic authentication stays disabled, so there is no publish profile. There is one environment and no staging slot. If Colima cannot start on the runner, the fallback is `ubuntu-latest` with configurable encoder paths, never a trimmed suite (012 research R-02, R-03; 012 decisions #2, #6, #7, #15).\n**Rationale**: Accepted from proposal P-03 by the owner. Tests on every push catch a broken branch before the local merge into `main`, and deploying only from `main` matches the owner's workflow of merging locally and pushing. OIDC means no long-lived secret exists to leak or rotate. The managed identity needs no directory rights, and the name-based subject matches because the repository's OIDC customization is the default (checked with `gh api`). Website Contributor on the one app limits what a compromised workflow can do; a change it makes to the app's settings is caught by `infra/check.sh` (D-24). The Basic plan has no deployment slots, and slots would need Standard S1, which exceeds Constitution VIII.1. The repository is public, so macOS runner minutes are free (012 research R-03).\n**Alternatives**: Running the workflow only on `main` was not chosen because branches would go untested until merge. Deploying from the owner's machine with `az webapp deploy` was not chosen because no test gate would stand between a commit and production; it remains the manual fallback. A publish profile or client secret in GitHub secrets was rejected: a long-lived password that needs basic authentication on. The portal's Continuous deployment was rejected because it commits its own workflow that deploys without our tests. An Entra app registration with a federated credential, the earlier choice, was replaced in feature 012: it is likely blocked in the company tenant, and the managed identity needs no directory rights. `environment: production` on the deploy job was rejected because it changes the OIDC subject (012 decisions #7).\n**Pivot cost**: low: the pipeline is one workflow file plus one managed identity; adding environments needs a second federated credential, and switching to manual `az webapp deploy` needs no infrastructure change (012 decisions #7).",
     1),
